Lower Bound
Problem Statement

We need to find the Lower Bound of a target element in a sorted array.

The Lower Bound is the index of the first element that is greater than or equal to the target.

If no such element exists, we will return n (the size of the array).

Approach

To do this we will follow these steps:

We will initialize two pointers:
low = 0
high = n - 1
We will maintain a variable answer = n.
We will calculate the middle index.
If the middle element is greater than or equal to the target:
We will store the current index as the answer.
Then we will search in the left half to find the first occurrence.
Otherwise, we will search in the right half.
Finally, we will return the answer.
Code
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

int LowerBound(int array[], int n, int target)
{
    int low=0;
    int high=n-1;
    int answer=n;

    while(low<=high)
    {
        int mid=low+(high-low)/2;

        if(array[mid]>=target)
        {
            answer=mid;
            high=mid-1;
        }
        else
        {
            low=mid+1;
        }
    }

    return answer;
}

int main()
{
    int array[]={1,2,4,4,5,6,8};
    int n=7;
    int target=4;

    cout<<LowerBound(array,n,target);

    return 0;
}
Example

If we have the array

1 2 4 4 5 6 8

and

Target = 4

The output will be

2

This is because the first element that is greater than or equal to 4 is at index 2.

Time Complexity

The time complexity of this solution is

O(log n)

This means that after every comparison, the search space is reduced by half.

Space Complexity

The space complexity of this solution is

O(1)

This means that only a few extra variables are used.

